---
/* DRAFT-FOR-ED — candidate node page, /next preview namespace. */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'
import MarkNote from '../../components/MarkNote.astro'

export const prerender = true
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Talking character"
  description="A drawn face taught to speak — generated, animated, and lip-synced on a home GPU."
>
  <article class="node">
    <p class="draft-stamp">draft · under review</p>
    <header data-mark="greg:masthead">
      <h1>Talking character</h1>
      <p class="dek">A drawn face taught to speak, end to end, on my own machine.</p>
      <p class="meta">craft · experiment · ComfyUI, Wan, MediaPipe</p>
    </header>

    <section data-mark="greg:what">
      <h2>What it is</h2>
      <p>
        Start with a 29-second audio monologue. Generate a portrait for the voice. Bring
        the portrait to life — the same face, holding its identity, breathing. Then sync
        its mouth to the recorded speech, word by word. Every stage runs on a consumer
        graphics card at home: no cloud rendering, no subscription, no one else's
        servers.
      </p>
      <p>
        The pipeline chains open video-generation models: one to paint the portrait, one
        to animate it while keeping the face consistent, one to drive the lips from
        audio. Long clips are stitched from short renders, which surfaced the hard bug —
        a lip-sync drift that accumulated across segments until the mouth ran ahead of
        the voice. The fix was exact tiling arithmetic between segment length and stride.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-mark="greg:capture">
      <h2>The capture side</h2>
      <p>
        A companion web app runs on a phone: it tracks the face in the browser — 52
        expression measurements per frame, on-device — while a teleprompter scrolls the
        script in time with the original audio. One real take produced 1,781 frames of
        usable expression data: jaw, smile, brow, blink. That data is the bridge to
        driving the character from a human performance instead of audio alone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section data-mark="greg:status">
      <h2>Where it stands</h2>
      <p>
        An experiment, honestly. The sync works; the render quality isn't yet where it
        should be, and each iteration is an hour of GPU time. Clips will appear here when
        one is worth showing.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <MarkNote />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .node {
    max-width: var(--measure);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-12) var(--page-gutter) var(--space-24);
  }
  .draft-stamp {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-8);
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--font-size-2xl);
    line-height: var(--leading-tight);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-3);
  }
  .dek {
    font-size: var(--font-size-md);
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
  }
  .meta {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-xs);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-8);
  }
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-4);
  }
  p {
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4);
  }
</style>
